---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import River from '../components/River.astro';
import ConfluenceMark from '../components/ConfluenceMark.astro';
import RiverLettering from '../components/RiverLettering.astro';
---
<Layout title="Tributary — A river-shaped identity study" description="Original Tributary identity studies exploring natural river shapes, confluences, and layered color.">
<div class="brand-study wrap">
  <header class="study-intro"><span class="eyebrow">TRIBUTARY / IDENTITY EXPLORATION</span><h1>A river can be<br />the whole language.</h1><p>Its bends shape the lettering. Its tributaries shape the diagram. Its colors give the flow a character.</p><p class="study-status">Design studies for review. The proposal’s purpose stays the same: royalties returning to creators.</p></header>
  <section class="study-logo" aria-labelledby="confluence-heading"><div class="study-notes"><span class="eyebrow">01 / CONFLUENCE</span><h2 id="confluence-heading">A small mark,<br />with a river inside.</h2><p>A meandering stem meets a tributary to suggest a lowercase “t.” The compact mark now anchors the navigation; its curves stay readable at small sizes.</p></div><div class="confluence-specimen"><div class="confluence-lockup"><ConfluenceMark size={92} /><span>tributary.</span></div><div class="mark-sizes"><ConfluenceMark size={24}/><ConfluenceMark size={40}/><ConfluenceMark size={64}/></div></div></section>
  <section class="study-logo" aria-labelledby="meander-heading"><div class="study-notes"><span class="eyebrow">02 / MEANDER</span><h2 id="meander-heading">Lettering shaped<br />by the current.</h2><p>Original river-like letterforms, with bends and loops carrying the name. A more expressive direction for a whitepaper cover or a larger brand moment.</p></div><div class="meander-specimen"><RiverLettering /><span class="specimen-caption">An expressive wordmark study</span></div></section>
  <section class="study-palette" aria-labelledby="palette-heading"><span class="eyebrow">03 / COLOR FROM THE LANDSCAPE</span><h2 id="palette-heading">Warm ground.<br />Living color.</h2><div class="palette-bands"><div class="palette-parchment"><span>Parchment</span></div><div class="palette-vermilion"><span>Vermilion</span></div><div class="palette-sediment"><span>Sediment</span></div><div class="palette-current"><span>Current</span></div><div class="palette-canopy"><span>Canopy</span></div></div><p>Parchment and forest give the page its light and dark rhythm. Vermilion, gold, cobalt, and olive bring warmth and detail to the streams. The palette takes its cue from the painting you shared.</p></section>
  <section class="study-flow" aria-labelledby="flow-heading"><div class="study-notes"><span class="eyebrow">04 / THE PROPOSED FLOW</span><h2 id="flow-heading">Natural bends.<br />Clear relationships.</h2><p>Layered ribbons bring the confluence into focus. Switch between royalties returning to your project and your code becoming part of other products.</p><p>The paths are illustrative. A measured Sankey chart would need verified amounts and widths proportional to those amounts.</p><a class="text-link" href="/">See it on the creator-focused homepage</a></div><River /></section>
  <section class="study-origin"><span class="eyebrow">REFERENCE TO REASONING</span><h2>The geometry does the work.</h2><p>Your river-lettering reference shows how landscape can supply the shapes of a name. <a href="https://www.metalab.com/work/windsurf">Metalab’s Windsurf identity</a> carries a natural motif across its mark, color, and larger applications. The <a href="https://tympanus.net/codrops/2025/11/17/windsurf-x-metalab-building-a-new-brand-for-the-future-of-ai-coding/">Codrops breakdown</a> helps explain how that motif becomes a wider system. These studies use original shapes for Tributary; they are inspired by river behavior rather than traced from satellite geography.</p><div class="study-film-notes"><article><h3>One shape, many uses.</h3><p>A confluence can connect the small mark, river diagram, and larger landscape artwork.</p></article><article><h3>Color needs a ground.</h3><p>Warm light surfaces carry reading. Forest backgrounds give the ribbons presence.</p></article><article><h3>Motion explains direction.</h3><p>The royalty switch changes the flow’s arrows. Reduced-motion preferences remove the transition.</p></article></div></section>
</div>
</Layout>
